time limit per test: 1.5 seconds
memory limit per test: 256 megabytes

You are given a positive integer n. A pair of non-negative integers a, b is called beautiful if the following conditions hold:

a + b = n.
The number a is a palindrome.*
The number b is divisible by 12.

You need to find a beautiful pair or report that it does not exist.

*A number is called a palindrome if and only if it remains the same when its digits (in decimal notation) are written in reverse order. For example, the numbers
12321, 6776, 5, 0 are palindromes, while the numbers 123 and 69 — are not.

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 ≤ t ≤ 10^4). The description of the test cases
follows.

The only line of each test case contains a single integer n (1 ≤ n ≤ 10^18).

Output:
For each test case, if such a and b exist, output a and b on a separate line, separated by a space. Otherwise, output -1 on a separate line.
If there are several beautiful pairs a, b, you may output any of them.

Strategy:
We get a+b=n where a is a palindrome and b mod 12 = 0. From this we know that a%12=n%12. This proves formally that we just need to check some 12 palindromes which correspond to the 12 possible asnwers to a%12. (0-11) Since 0-9 are palindromes and 11 is a palindrome we use that. The first palindrome where a%12=10 is 22. We can just check if n%12=10. If it is we output 22 and n-22. If n%12==10 and n<22 there is no solution. For any other n%12 we can just output n%12 n-n%12.